<urn:kg:jardiance:pisection:75c96592-f767-4df2-9e70-b76cf412ebf1> a kg:PISection ;
    rdfs:label "jardiance-epar-product-information_en.pdf" ;
    kg:DERIVED_FROM_DOCUMENT <urn:kg:jardiance:document:98de1eab-c096-475d-9a30-ff5319cefc40> ;
    kg:DERIVED_FROM_SECTION <urn:kg:jardiance:section:75c96592-f767-4df2-9e70-b76cf412ebf1> ;
    kg:PI_SECTION_CONTAINS_SAFETY <urn:kg:jardiance:safety:75c96592-f767-4df2-9e70-b76cf412ebf1> ;
    kg:graphScope "brand" ;
    kg:page_number 13 ;
    kg:section_id "75c96592-f767-4df2-9e70-b76cf412ebf1" ;
    kg:section_title "Monotherapy" ;
    kg:source_document_id "98de1eab-c096-475d-9a30-ff5319cefc40" ;
    kg:source_document_type "product_information" ;
    kg:source_file_name "jardiance-epar-product-information_en.pdf" ;
    kg:text """The efficacy and safety of empagliflozin as monotherapy was evaluated in a double-blind, placebo- and active-controlled study of 24 weeks duration in treatment-naïve patients. Treatment with empagliflozin resulted in a statistically significant (p &lt; 0.0001) reduction in HbA1c compared to placebo (Table 2) and a clinically meaningful decrease in FPG.

In a pre-specified analysis of patients (N = 201) with a baseline HbA1c ≥ 8.5%, treatment resulted in a reduction in HbA1c from baseline of -1.44% for empagliflozin 10 mg, -1.43% for empagliflozin 25 mg, -1.04% for sitagliptin, and an increase of 0.01% for placebo.""" .

<urn:kg:jardiance:pisection:75e24a75-eeca-44c9-8778-473889172003> a kg:PISection ;
    rdfs:label "Jardiance_PI.pdf" ;
    kg:DERIVED_FROM_DOCUMENT <urn:kg:jardiance:document:b2560bb2-851a-4e06-8246-07641712e8b0> ;
    kg:DERIVED_FROM_SECTION <urn:kg:jardiance:section:75e24a75-eeca-44c9-8778-473889172003> ;
    kg:graphScope "brand" ;
    kg:page_number 16 ;
    kg:section_id "75e24a75-eeca-44c9-8778-473889172003" ;
    kg:section_title "12.3  Pharmacokinetics" ;
    kg:source_document_id "b2560bb2-851a-4e06-8246-07641712e8b0" ;
    kg:source_document_type "prescribing_information" ;
    kg:source_file_name "Jardiance_PI.pdf" ;
    kg:text "The pharmacokinetics of empagliflozin has been characterized in healthy volunteers and patients with type 2 diabetes mellitus and no clinically relevant differences were noted between the two populations.  The steadystate mean plasma AUC and Cmax were 1,870 nmol ā h/L and 259 nmol/L, respectively, with 10 mg empagliflozin once daily treatment, and 4,740 nmol ā h/L and 687 nmol/L, respectively, with 25 mg empagliflozin once daily treatment.  Systemic exposure of empagliflozin increased in a dose-proportional manner in the therapeutic dose range. Empagliflozin does not appear to have time-dependent pharmacokinetic characteristics. Following oncedaily dosing, up to 22% accumulation, with respect to plasma AUC, was observed at steady-state." .

<urn:kg:jardiance:pisection:77d73b63-98f8-4f3b-b9ad-840ab3692dc6> a kg:PISection ;
    rdfs:label "jardiance-epar-product-information_en.pdf" ;
    kg:DERIVED_FROM_DOCUMENT <urn:kg:jardiance:document:98de1eab-c096-475d-9a30-ff5319cefc40> ;
    kg:DERIVED_FROM_SECTION <urn:kg:jardiance:section:77d73b63-98f8-4f3b-b9ad-840ab3692dc6> ;
    kg:graphScope "brand" ;
    kg:page_number 6 ;
    kg:section_id "77d73b63-98f8-4f3b-b9ad-840ab3692dc6" ;
    kg:section_title "Interference with 1,5-anhydroglucitol (1,5-AG) assay" ;
    kg:source_document_id "98de1eab-c096-475d-9a30-ff5319cefc40" ;
    kg:source_document_type "product_information" ;
    kg:source_file_name "jardiance-epar-product-information_en.pdf" ;
    kg:text "Monitoring glycaemic control with 1,5-AG assay is not recommended as measurements of 1,5-AG are unreliable in assessing glycaemic control in patients taking SGLT2 inhibitors. Use of alternative methods to monitor glycaemic control is advised." .

